Hi {{FirstName}},

If your CFO found every cloud workload running on the most expensive instance available, how long would that last?

About a day.

Yet that's how many companies run AI. Summaries, extraction, classification, translation and agent loops all go through premium frontier models because those models are available, not because the task needs them.

At scale this gets expensive fast. Menlo Ventures estimates enterprise GenAI spend reached $37B in 2025, 3.2x the year before. Agents multiply it: one business action can trigger hundreds of model calls.

The fix is model routing, which is really AI FinOps:

- ~60% everyday work → smaller or open-weight models
- ~30% business-critical → the best cost-performance model that meets your control threshold
- ~10% high-stakes → frontier models

Your mix will differ. The principle won't. For suitable workloads, costs can drop 70–90%.

And the board metric isn't cost per million tokens. It's cost per correct business outcome.

I've had close access across the major US, European and Asian AI labs, and I'm not paid to defend any one model family. I filmed what I saw inside the Chinese labs, including how they design for cost from day one:

→ Watch the China AI Tour: https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLe9FR2buoEnM

If this is a board-level conversation for you, just reply "ROUTING".

Danilo

P.S. In August, Reuters reported that one DeepSeek flagship variant was more than 100x cheaper to run on benchmark tests than one Anthropic model. That's not a reason to switch vendors. It's a sign that frontier-only architecture is getting hard to defend. The tour covers what's behind that gap: https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLe9FR2buoEnM

